<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="021" project="spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI">
  <metadata>
    <title>Build ordered credential selection — the rollback mechanism (FR-B2)</title>
    <phase>2 Provider</phase>
    <gate>020 complete</gate>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>auth tag, modifies .cs; this IS the rollback mechanism NFR-06 depends on.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Well-specified once the seam exists; the reference behaviour is documented in ADR-028 A4.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>C</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <deps>020</deps>
    <tags>auth,bff-api,config</tags>
    <estimated-effort>4-6 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Build config-driven ordered credential selection into the provider — MI-FIC, then Key Vault certificate, then dev secret — so that reordering the configured list changes the selected credential with no code change. This is what makes rollback config-only at every phase.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Follow ADR-028 A4.</role>
  <goal>An ordered, config-bound credential selector with tested fall-through, so a rollback is an app-settings edit rather than a redeploy.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>E4' established the declarative Microsoft.Identity.Web ClientCredentials list is unavailable in this codebase, so the ordered fallback the whole rollback story rests on must be BUILT. Design §6 states rollback at every phase is a credential reorder or a slot swap back; without this task that claim is false.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Auth/ManagedIdentityAssertionProvider.cs</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Graph/CiamGraphClientFactory.cs</file>
      <file role="new">tests/integration/seam/Auth/CredentialOrderingSeamTests.cs</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies><dependency task="020" status="pending">The provider seam</dependency></dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-028">Scope: order is MI-FIC, then KV certificate, then dev secret. NEVER promote a secret above a secret-free credential.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: THIS TASK MUST AUTHOR THE CLIENT-LEVEL SEAM. Booked by task 020 (adr-check finding V1). Ordered selection CANNOT be expressed behind IClientAssertionProvider: its contract is Task&lt;string&gt; GetAssertionAsync(...), and of the three credentials only MI-FIC yields an assertion -- a KV certificate uses .WithCertificate(x509) and a secret uses .WithClientSecret(...). Two of three branches have no assertion to return. Widening the assertion contract to cover them would produce a type whose name is a lie. Declare a SECOND contract in Spaarke.Dataverse (e.g. IConfidentialClientProvider) that hands back a configured IConfidentialClientApplication, owns the ordered selection internally, and consumes IClientAssertionProvider for the MI-FIC branch only. Spaarke.Dataverse already references Microsoft.Identity.Client 4.87.0, so an MSAL-typed member is legal there and adds NO package reference (FR-14 unaffected -- verify).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-028">Scope: that second contract's implementation is ALSO the home of the single shared confidential-client cache, keyed (tenant|client|credential-kind) -- credential-kind because the selected credential is bound into the client at Build(). This is what lets task 022 collapse the three per-class CCA caches and CLOSE task 011's time-boxed A4 exception. Note only ONE of the four call sites (DataverseAccessDataSource) actually needs the contract; the three BFF-side sites can inject the cache CONCRETELY, which ADR-010 prefers -- do not add interfaces they do not need.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: a missing or failing higher-priority credential falls through to the next WITH A LOGGED WARNING. It must not throw and must not silently skip without a trace.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: FALL-THROUGH IS NOT UNIFORM -- own the decision in ONE predicate. Booked by task 020 (code review W-9). MSAL 4.87 throws at least four relevant codes and they do NOT all mean the same thing: managed_identity_unreachable_network (no IMDS route -- FALL THROUGH, this is local dev) vs managed_identity_request_failed (IMDS reachable but the identity is absent/wrong -- this is the FR-B4 wrong-identity signature and MUST FAIL LOUD; falling through here would silently run production on the secret while looking healthy) vs managed_identity_all_sources_unavailable vs user_assigned_managed_identity_not_supported. Put the code list in ONE predicate on the provider (e.g. IsFallThroughEligible(MsalServiceException)) so it is testable in one place and cannot be re-derived differently at each consumer. Do NOT add an IsAvailable probe to the interface -- a probe is a second network call that races the real one.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: NEGATIVE-CACHE the failures. ClientAssertionProviderBase caches successes only, so a failing mint is retried on EVERY token acquisition -- ~80ms per request off-Azure (measured at task 020), and MSAL's full retry ladder per request during the AADSTS70021 FIC-propagation window ADR-028 A4 warns about. Add a short-TTL negative memo.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the certificate branch reuses the proven CiamGraphClientFactory.cs:154-170 KV PFX load (private key ephemeral in-process). Do not write a second certificate loader.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: coverage to tests/integration/seam/**; assert selection behaviour, not container wiring.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>Ordered credential selection, KV certificates</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-028-spaarke-auth-architecture.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/auth.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI/notes/RESEARCH-FINDINGS.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step>Define the config shape for an ordered credential list (bound via Options, ValidateOnStart-friendly).</step>
    <step>Implement selection with fall-through and warning-level logging on each skip.</step>
    <step>Wire the certificate branch to the existing CiamGraphClientFactory KV PFX loader.</step>
    <step>Add seam tests covering each ordering permutation and each fall-through.</step>
    <step>Document the config keys in the project notes for the Phase 3 runbook.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>Ordered selection cannot be made config-only without a restart — record it; NFR-06 says "configuration change", and a restart is acceptable, but a redeploy is NOT.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Auth/ManagedIdentityAssertionProvider.cs</output>
    <output type="new">tests/integration/seam/Auth/CredentialOrderingSeamTests.cs</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI/notes/decisions/021-credential-config-keys.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>Reordering the configured credential list changes the selected credential with no code change and no redeploy.</criterion>
    <criterion>Each fall-through emits a warning naming the skipped credential and the reason.</criterion>
    <criterion>Negative case: an empty credential list fails fast at startup with an actionable message.</criterion>
    <criterion>managed_identity_request_failed (wrong/absent identity, FR-B4) FAILS LOUD rather than falling through to the secret -- asserted, with a negative control proving the eligible codes DO fall through.</criterion>
    <criterion>Decide and record whether IClientAssertionProvider widens to a Spaarke-owned request record (ClientId, TokenEndpoint, TenantId -- no MSAL types). Booked by task 020 (code review Q4): the contract currently drops TokenEndpoint and ClientID, which a Key Vault CERTIFICATE assertion needs for its aud/iss/sub, and ClientAssertionProviderBase caches options-blind -- correct for MI-FIC, a correctness bug for a certificate. Not required while MI-FIC is the only credential; must be decided before six call sites bind at task 022.</criterion>
    <criterion>Negative case: a configured-but-broken MI-FIC falls through to the next credential rather than throwing.</criterion>
    <criterion>A client-level contract exists in Spaarke.Dataverse that returns a configured confidential client, and its implementation owns exactly ONE confidential-client cache. Without it task 022 cannot consolidate and task 011's A4 exception becomes permanent.</criterion>
    <criterion>Negative case: a secret configured ABOVE a secret-free credential is rejected or logged as a misconfiguration, per ADR-028 A4.</criterion>
    <criterion>Publish size reported; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
